    slug: "denture-repair-vs-replacement-how-to-know-when-it-is-time-for-new-dentures",
    title: "Denture Repair vs Replacement: How to Know When it is Time for New Dentures",
    description: "Learn the signs that indicate whether you need denture repair or replacement, and when it’s time to consider new dentures in Ottawa.",
    date: "2025-08-12",
    image: "/orleans/c2135009_Denture-Repair-scaled.jpeg",
    imageAlt: "Denture Repair vs Replacement: How to Know When it is Time for New Dentures",
    html: "<h2 id=\"denture-repair-vs-replacement-how-to-know-when-it-is-time-for-new-dentures\">Denture Repair vs Replacement: How to Know When it is Time for New Dentures</h2><p>Denture wearers often face the question of whether to repair or replace their dentures. Denture Repair vs. Replacement: How to Know When it is Time for New Dentures is an important topic for anyone looking to maintain their oral health and comfort. This guide will help you understand the signs of damage, when repairs are a good option, and when a full replacement is the better choice. You’ll also learn about standard repair methods, the lifespan of dentures, and how to keep them in the best condition possible.</p><h2 id=\"signs-your-dentures-may-need-repair\">Signs Your Dentures May Need Repair</h2><p>Dentures can withstand daily use, but accidents, natural wear, and changes in your mouth can lead to issues. <a href=\"https://clinicdenture.com/blog/6-signs-your-dentures-need-a-reline-or-repair/\">Common signs</a> that you may need denture repair include small cracks or chips, a single tooth breaking off, or looseness in the fit. If your dentures feel uncomfortable or rub against your gums, adjustments can often restore comfort. Denture repair can also address problems like damaged clasps on partial dentures or wear on the bite surface. In many cases, same-day dentures or quick fixes can extend the life of your current set without the need for a replacement.</p><h3 id=\"when-denture-replacement-is-the-better-option\">When Denture Replacement is the Better Option</h3><p>While repairs are effective for minor damage, there are times when replacement is unavoidable. Dentures typically last between five to ten years, depending on the material and care. Over time, your gums and jawbone can change shape, making a proper fit impossible with the old set. If your dentures have multiple cracks, extensive wear, or no longer fit even after relines for dentures, it may be time for new ones. Replacement is also recommended if you have frequent sore spots, difficulty speaking, or chewing. For many patients in Ottawa, a denture consultation is the first step to assessing whether a replacement is the right choice.</p><h3 id=\"factors-to-consider-before-deciding\">Factors to Consider Before Deciding</h3><p>Deciding between repair and replacement depends on your denture’s age, the extent of the damage, and your oral health needs. If the damage is minor and the dentures are relatively new, a repair can be cost-effective. For older dentures, the cost of repeated repairs may outweigh the investment in new dentures. Your lifestyle also matters. For example, if you rely on your dentures for speaking and social interactions daily, a poorly fitting or damaged denture can impact your confidence. Consulting a denturist ensures you get professional advice tailored to your needs and budget. In Ottawa, denture services often include both repair and replacement options, giving you flexibility.</p><p><a href=\"/blog/dentures-101\">On the Blog – Dentures 101: The Types, Benefits, and What to Expect</a></p><h2 id=\"maintaining-dentures-to-extend-their-life\">Maintaining Dentures to Extend Their Life</h2><h2 id=\"did-you-know\">Did You Know?</h2><p>A study published in the <a href=\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0022034514552493\"><em>Journal of Dental Research</em></a>found that sleeping with dentures increases the risk of pneumonia by <strong>2.3 times</strong> in older adults, highlighting the importance of proper overnight storage and denture hygiene.</p><h2 id=\"conclusion-making-the-right-choice-for-your-smile\">Conclusion: Making the Right Choice for Your Smile</h2><p>Denture Repair vs. Replacement: How to Know When it is Time for New Dentures comes down to the condition of your dentures, their age, and how they fit. Minor damage can often be fixed, while older, ill-fitting dentures may require a complete replacement to restore comfort and function. Regular check-ups and proper care can extend the life of your dentures, but when the time comes, a professional denturist can guide you toward the best solution. At <a href=\"/our-lab\">Orleans Denture Clinic,</a> we help patients make informed decisions that support long-term oral health.</p><h2 id=\"get-expert-denture-care-in-orleans\">Get Expert Denture Care in Orleans</h2><p>At Orleans Denture Clinic, we provide <a href=\"/services/dentures\">comprehensive denture services</a>, including repairs, relines, and full replacements. Whether you need <a href=\"/services/immediate-dentures\">same-day dentures</a>, partial dentures, or an entirely new set, our team is here to ensure you get the perfect fit. We offer personalized consultations to assess your needs and recommend the best solution for your comfort and confidence.</p><p><a href=\"/contact-us\">Contact us today</a> to book your appointment and get back to smiling with ease.</p><h3 id=\"get-a-free-consultation\">Get a Free Consultation</h3><p>Book your free consultation, and get in touch with a denturist expert, today!</p>",
